WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.260
Injection is a design failure where untrusted input changes the meaning of something the application sends to an interpreter.

2
00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:25.960
In C# and .NET systems, that interpreter might be a SQL database, an ORM query layer, an LDAP directory, an XML parser, a template renderer, an expression evaluator, a shell, a browser, a queue consumer, or a downstream API.

3
00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:34.780
The dangerous moment is when data stops being treated as data and begins to shape commands, filters, expressions, documents, or protocol behavior.

4
00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:38.580
This is broader than the classic idea of SQL injection.

5
00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:54.120
A value from a request can influence a database query, but it can also influence a file path, a process argument, an OData filter, an XPath expression, a Razor rendering decision, a JSON model binding path, or an outbound API call.

6
00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:56.940
Every one of those contexts has its own rules.

7
00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:02.800
One escaping function, one validator, or one framework feature cannot be the complete answer.

8
00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:06.200
The controls are related, but not interchangeable.

9
00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:10.780
Validation checks whether input matches expected business intent.

10
00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:15.040
Parameterization keeps values separate from query syntax.

11
00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:19.060
Output encoding makes text safe for a specific browser context.

12
00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:24.120
Allowlisting limits choices to known fields, operations, or formats.

13
00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:30.060
Safe API design avoids passing raw strings into dangerous interpreters in the first place.

14
00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:33.840
Strong prevention usually combines several of these controls.

15
00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:37.800
The core goal is to keep data as data.

16
00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:43.020
A customer name should remain a value, not become part of a query expression.

17
00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:47.840
A report format should map to a known operation, not select an arbitrary command.

18
00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:52.340
A comment should render as content, not become HTML or template logic.

19
00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:59.960
This course focuses on the engineering habits that help C# developers recognize sensitive sinks and choose the right control before input reaches them.
